  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby* by F. Scott Fitzgerald**\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald\u2019s *The Great Gatsby* is a timeless novel that captures the essence of the American Dream and its inevitable collapse under the weight of illusion and desire. Set in the 1920s, the story follows the enigmatic Jay Gatsby, a wealthy man who throws lavish parties in hopes of rekindling a lost love, Daisy Buchanan. The narrative, told from the perspective of Nick Carraway, reveals the deep-seated cynicism and moral decay that lies beneath the glittering surface of the Jazz Age.\n\nThe novel is rich with symbolism, most notably the green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock, which represents Gatsby\u2019s hopes and dreams. The Eyes of T.J. Eckleburg, looming over the Valley of Ashes, serve as a reminder of the moral decay and spiritual emptiness that pervade the characters' lives. The recurring motif of the color yellow, associated with both wealth and corruption, further underscores the novel\u2019s central themes.\n\nFitzgerald masterfully contrasts the opulence of East Egg with the stark poverty of West Egg, highlighting the social divides of the time. The tragic romance between Gatsby and Daisy is not just a personal story, but a reflection of the larger societal failures of the 1920s. Gatsby\u2019s relentless pursuit of the past and his inability to accept reality ultimately lead to his downfall, making the novel a poignant commentary on the futility of idealism.\n\nKey characters such as Tom Buchanan, with his brute strength and intolerance, and Jordan Baker, the independent and cynical love interest, further illustrate the moral complexity of the era. The setting of New York City, particularly the opulent parties at Gatsby\u2019s mansion, serves as a backdrop for the novel\u2019s exploration of decadence and its consequences.\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is not just a love story, but a social critique of the American Dream. Fitzgerald\u2019s vivid descriptions and lyrical prose make it a masterpiece of American literature, and its themes remain startlingly relevant even today.\n\n**Proper Nouns:**\n1. The Great Gatsby\n2. F. Scott Fitzgerald\n3. Jay Gatsby\n4. Daisy Buchanan\n5. Nick Carraway\n6. T.J. Eckleburg\n7. East Egg\n8. West Egg\n9. Valley of Ashes\n10. New York City\n11. Jordan Baker\n12. Tom Buchanan\n13. Gatsby\u2019s mansion\n\nTotal: **13 proper nouns**.",
